<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="60–70 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson 4</p>
  <h1><span class="ex">Used to</span> and <span class="ex">would</span>: <em>build a past world, then enter its routines</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst"><span class="ex">Used to</span> can establish an old state or repeated habit and normally invites a contrast with now. Habitual <span class="ex">would</span> lets a speaker revisit repeated actions once the past world is clear. Past Simple remains available for routines and moves the story to one finished event.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">60–70 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">past state · repeated action · one event</span>
    <span class="tag">used to + base · would + base</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Separate background states, repeated scenes, and one event.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Choose a form from its discourse job, not one signal word.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Contrast</b><span>Keep old routines separate from being or becoming familiar.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Create a fictional oral history with a clear then-and-now arc.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can form positive, negative, question, and <span class="ex">there used to be</span> patterns; use <span class="ex">used to</span> for past states or habits that contrast with now; use habitual <span class="ex">would</span> for repeated actions inside a recoverable past frame; use Past Simple for a neutral routine or one event; distinguish possession states from repeated event meanings; separate <span class="ex">used to + base verb</span> from <span class="ex">be/get used to + noun or -ing</span>; hear connected U.S. forms; and tell a coherent then-and-now story.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved a repeated Past Simple routine, a continuing state, and one dated finished event.">
    <h3>Three foundations from A2</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the stated frequency, verb meaning, and event boundary.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Past habit foundation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="opened">opened</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="owned">owned</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="closed">closed</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="was opening tomorrow">was opening tomorrow</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="bought each year">bought each year</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="closes">closes</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The old bakery followed this routine every weekday in 2008: It <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="opened" data-hint="Past Simple can describe a repeated past routine when every weekday supplies the frequency." data-fix="The bakery opened at 6:00 every weekday in 2008.">choose</button> at 6:00 every weekday.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>From 2002 to 2014, Lena possessed the bakery: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="owned" data-hint="Own presents a continuing possession state across the stated finished period." data-fix="Lena owned the bakery from 2002 to 2014.">choose</button> the bakery.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>One storm ended the business on September 14, 2014: The bakery <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="closed" data-hint="Use Past Simple for the single finished event on the exact date." data-fix="The bakery closed on September 14, 2014.">choose</button> on that date.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice the background, repeated scene, and one event</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Riverside neighborhood audio archive</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">There used to be a bakery beside the station. Lena used to own it, and the front room used to be bright yellow.</span></p>
    <p><span class="ex">Every weekday, she would unlock the door at six. She would put two chairs outside and greet the first commuters.</span></p>
    <p><span class="ex">One Saturday in 2014, a storm damaged the roof. The bakery closed that month. A language center occupies the building now.</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which forms establish past states that are different now?</li>
      <li>Which repeated actions happen inside the established weekday frame?</li>
      <li>Which verbs move the account to one finished event?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You recovered the former bakery, possession state, repeated weekday actions, one storm event, and present-day contrast.">
    <h3>Discovery check: identify each form’s job</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Used to and would discovery choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a bakery beside the station">a bakery beside the station</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Lena owned it">Lena owned it</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="unlock and greet">unlock and greet</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a storm damaged the roof">a storm damaged the roof</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a language center occupies it now">a language center occupies it now</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The former place introduced by <span class="ex">There used to be</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a bakery beside the station" data-hint="Read the first clause for the place that existed there before the language center." data-fix="There used to be a bakery beside the station.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The continuing possession state in the old period is that <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Lena owned it" data-hint="Find the used to clause with the possession verb own." data-fix="Lena used to own the bakery.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Two repeated weekday actions expressed with habitual <span class="ex">would</span> are <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="unlock and greet" data-hint="Use the actions that recurred every weekday, not the single storm event." data-fix="Every weekday, Lena would unlock the door and greet commuters.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The single event that changes the story is that <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a storm damaged the roof" data-hint="Look for the event attached to one Saturday in 2014." data-fix="One Saturday in 2014, a storm damaged the roof.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The explicit present contrast is that <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a language center occupies it now" data-hint="Choose the final sentence with the time marker now." data-fix="A language center occupies the building now.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: choose a discourse job</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Used to</span> presents a past state or repeated habit and normally invites the listener to understand that the situation is different now. The contrast may be stated, as in <span class="ex">It used to be a bakery, but it is a language center now</span>, or strongly implied by context.</p>
  <p>Habitual <span class="ex">would</span> revisits repeated actions inside a recoverable past frame. The frame can come earlier in the story or in the same sentence: <span class="ex">Back in 2008, Lena would unlock the door at six</span>. It often makes a memory feel like a sequence of recurring scenes. It does not by itself emphasize a contrast with now.</p>
  <p>Past Simple is not merely the “one-event form.” It can also describe a state or routine when time and frequency are clear: <span class="ex">The bakery opened at six every weekday</span>. Choose it for neutral reporting or for the specific finished events that advance a story.</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>job</th><th>form</th><th>complete example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>positive past state or habit</td><td><span class="ex">subject + used to + base verb</span></td><td><span class="ex">Lena used to own the bakery.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>negative past state or habit</td><td><span class="ex">subject + didn’t use to + base verb</span></td><td><span class="ex">The shop didn’t use to open Sundays.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>question</td><td><span class="ex">Did + subject + use to + base verb?</span></td><td><span class="ex">Did it use to open early?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>former existence</td><td><span class="ex">There used to be + noun phrase</span></td><td><span class="ex">There used to be a bakery here.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>repeated action in a past frame</td><td><span class="ex">subject + would + base verb</span></td><td><span class="ex">Every morning, Lena would greet commuters.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You built positive, negative, question, former-existence, and habitual-would forms with the correct base verb.">
    <h3>Form check: keep the verb chain accurate</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Used to and would form choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="used to own">used to own</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="didn’t use to open">didn’t use to open</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Did the bakery use to sell">Did the bakery use to sell</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="There used to be">There used to be</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would greet">would greet</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Possession was true from 2002 to 2014 and is not true now: Lena <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="used to own" data-hint="Use used to plus the base verb own for the former possession state." data-fix="Lena used to own the bakery.">choose</button> the bakery.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Sunday opening was not part of the old routine: The bakery <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="didn’t use to open" data-hint="Didn’t carries the past marking, so use stays in its base form before to open." data-fix="The bakery didn’t use to open on Sundays.">choose</button> on Sundays.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Ask about a former product that may be different now: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Did the bakery use to sell" data-hint="Begin with Did, keep the subject bakery before use to, and use sell as the base verb." data-fix="Did the bakery use to sell bread after noon?">choose</button> bread after noon?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>State that a small market formerly existed beside the bakery: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="There used to be" data-hint="Use There used to be before the noun phrase a small market." data-fix="There used to be a small market beside the bakery.">choose</button> a small market beside the bakery.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The frame <span class="ex">Every weekday in 2008</span> makes the repeated action clear: Lena <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="would greet" data-hint="Use would plus the base verb greet for the repeated action inside the stated past frame." data-fix="Every weekday in 2008, Lena would greet the first commuters.">choose</button> the first commuters.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Choose <span class="ex">used to</span>, habitual <span class="ex">would</span>, or Past Simple</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Sometimes more than one form is grammatical. A strong exercise must therefore state the discourse goal. Compare:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>speaker’s goal</th><th>choice</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>establish a former state and now-contrast</td><td><span class="ex">used to</span></td><td><span class="ex">The front room used to be yellow.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>evoke a repeated scene after the frame is clear</td><td>habitual <span class="ex">would</span></td><td><span class="ex">Each morning, Lena would arrange the bread.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>report a neutral routine</td><td>Past Simple</td><td><span class="ex">The bakery opened at six every weekday.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>advance to one event</td><td>Past Simple</td><td><span class="ex">A storm damaged the roof in 2014.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used used to for explicit change, would for recurring scenes, and Past Simple for neutral routine and one event according to the requested discourse goal.">
    <h3>Choice check: follow the requested storytelling job</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Used to would and past simple choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="used to be">used to be</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would arrange">would arrange</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="opened">opened</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="damaged">damaged</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="used to hold">used to hold</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Establish a former color and contrast it with the white room now: The front room <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="used to be" data-hint="The prompt requests a former state with an explicit now-contrast." data-fix="The front room used to be yellow, but it is white now.">choose</button> yellow, but it is white now.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The 2008 weekday frame is already clear. Evoke one recurring scene: Each morning, Lena <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="would arrange" data-hint="Use habitual would to revisit a repeated action inside the established past frame." data-fix="Each morning, Lena would arrange the bread near the window.">choose</button> the bread near the window.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The archive label needs a neutral factual routine, not a then-and-now contrast: The bakery <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="opened" data-hint="Past Simple can report this routine neutrally because every weekday supplies repetition." data-fix="The bakery opened at 6:00 every weekday.">choose</button> at 6:00 every weekday.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Move the story from routine to one dated event: On September 14, 2014, a storm <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="damaged" data-hint="Use Past Simple for the one finished event on the exact date." data-fix="On September 14, 2014, a storm damaged the roof.">choose</button> the roof.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Explicitly contrast the former monthly classes with the absence of classes now: The bakery <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="used to hold" data-hint="The prompt asks for a repeated habit presented as different from now." data-fix="The bakery used to hold a bread-making class each month, but no classes happen there now.">choose</button> a bread-making class each month.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>State or repeated event? Meaning comes first</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>For a simple continuing past state, <span class="ex">used to</span> is the safest B1 choice: <span class="ex">She used to own the bakery. She used to know every customer. The room used to be yellow.</span> Habitual <span class="ex">would</span> normally foregrounds repeated events rather than a plain continuing state.</p>
  <p>Do not classify a written verb forever. Meaning can change. <span class="ex">She used to have a delivery van</span> presents possession, a state. <span class="ex">Every Friday, she would have lunch with the suppliers</span> presents a repeated event. Likewise, <span class="ex">She used to think the station was noisy</span> presents an opinion state, while <span class="ex">After closing, she would think about new recipes</span> presents a repeated mental activity.</p>
  <p>Literary and expressive English sometimes uses <span class="ex">would</span> with a characteristic situation, as in <span class="ex">On winter mornings, the room would be freezing</span>. This lesson does not label that use impossible. For a neutral, continuous state with a clear then-and-now contrast, <span class="ex">used to</span> remains the more dependable learner choice.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished possession and opinion states from repeated travel and meal events by meaning rather than by memorizing a permanent verb list.">
    <h3>Meaning check: state or recurring event?</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Past state and repeated event choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="used to own">used to own</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="used to think">used to think</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would drive">would drive</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would have">would have</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Possession continued across the old period and is different now: Lena <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="used to own" data-hint="Own presents possession as a continuing state in this sentence." data-fix="Lena used to own the bakery.">choose</button> the bakery.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The sentence presents one continuing opinion that later changed: Lena <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="used to think" data-hint="Think presents an opinion state here, not repeated acts of considering." data-fix="Lena used to think the station was too noisy.">choose</button> the station was too noisy.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The Friday frame turns <span class="ex">drive</span> into a recurring delivery event: Every Friday, Lena <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="would drive" data-hint="Use habitual would for the repeated action inside the clear Friday frame." data-fix="Every Friday, Lena would drive to the wholesale market.">choose</button> to the wholesale market.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span><span class="ex">Have lunch</span> names a recurring event, not possession: After the market trip, she <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="would have" data-hint="The established Friday routine makes have lunch a repeated event." data-fix="After the market trip, she would have lunch with the suppliers.">choose</button> lunch with the suppliers.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Do not confuse an old routine with familiarity</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>meaning</th><th>form</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>old state or habit, normally different now</td><td><span class="ex">used to + base verb</span></td><td><span class="ex">Maya used to work afternoons.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>familiar or accustomed now</td><td><span class="ex">be used to + noun or -ing</span></td><td><span class="ex">Maya is used to working early.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>become familiar over time</td><td><span class="ex">get used to + noun or -ing</span></td><td><span class="ex">Maya got used to the early schedule.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>purpose of an object</td><td><span class="ex">be used for + noun or -ing</span></td><td><span class="ex">This room is used for recording.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>In the old-habit form, <span class="ex">to</span> belongs to the infinitive and is followed by a base verb: <span class="ex">used to work</span>. In <span class="ex">be/get used to</span>, <span class="ex">to</span> is a preposition and is followed by a noun or <span class="ex">-ing</span> form: <span class="ex">used to the schedule, used to working early</span>.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated a former work schedule, present familiarity, the process of becoming familiar, and the room’s purpose.">
    <h3>Boundary check: old habit, familiarity, adjustment, or purpose?</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Used to and familiarity choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="used to work">used to work</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="is used to working">is used to working</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="got used to working">got used to working</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="is used for recording">is used for recording</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Maya worked afternoons in her old role, but she does not now: Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="used to work" data-hint="Use used to plus the base verb work for the former schedule." data-fix="Maya used to work afternoons.">choose</button> afternoons.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Early work feels normal to Maya now: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="is used to working" data-hint="Use be used to plus working for present familiarity with the activity." data-fix="Maya is used to working early now.">choose</button> early now.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>During her first month, the early schedule gradually became familiar: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="got used to working" data-hint="Use got used to plus working for the completed adjustment process." data-fix="During her first month, Maya got used to working early.">choose</button> early.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The sentence explains the current function of the audio room: It <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="is used for recording" data-hint="Use is used for plus recording to state the room’s purpose." data-fix="The audio room is used for recording interviews.">choose</button> interviews.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Questions, negatives, and the meaning of <span class="ex">wouldn’t</span></h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>In standard edited English, <span class="ex">did</span> carries past marking, so write <span class="ex">Did you use to...?</span> and <span class="ex">didn’t use to...</span>. The positive form remains <span class="ex">used to</span>. Speech often makes <span class="ex">used to</span> and <span class="ex">use to</span> sound alike, so use the auxiliary to guide the spelling.</p>
  <p>For a neutral absence of an old habit, <span class="ex">didn’t use to</span> is usually clearest: <span class="ex">The bakery didn’t use to open Sundays.</span> Habitual <span class="ex">wouldn’t</span> often suggests refusal or unwillingness: <span class="ex">Even when customers asked, Lena wouldn’t sell the display cake.</span> Context decides whether the negative describes simple non-occurrence or repeated refusal.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You formed the standard edited negative, question, and short answer, and used wouldn’t only for the stated repeated refusal.">
    <h3>Form and meaning check: let <span class="ex">did</span> carry past time</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Used to question and negative choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="didn’t use to open">didn’t use to open</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Did Lena use to sell">Did Lena use to sell</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="she did">she did</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="wouldn’t sell">wouldn’t sell</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Sunday opening was simply absent from the old schedule: The bakery <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="didn’t use to open" data-hint="Use didn’t use to for a neutral absent habit, then keep open in the base form." data-fix="The bakery didn’t use to open on Sundays.">choose</button> on Sundays.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Ask about a former product: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Did Lena use to sell" data-hint="Begin with Did and use the base spelling use after the subject Lena." data-fix="Did Lena use to sell soup at lunchtime?">choose</button> soup at lunchtime?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The archive confirms the positive answer to that question: “Yes, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="she did" data-hint="A short answer repeats the subject and auxiliary did." data-fix="Yes, she did.">choose</button>.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Customers repeatedly requested the display cake, but Lena refused every time: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="wouldn’t sell" data-hint="The context explicitly describes repeated refusal, so wouldn’t is meaningful here." data-fix="Even when customers asked, Lena wouldn’t sell the display cake.">choose</button> the display cake.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Build the past world, revisit routines, then move the story</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A coherent memory is more than a list of forms. Establish the period and background, enter its repeated scenes, move to a specific event, and close with the present contrast.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>discourse step</th><th>useful choice</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>establish the past world</td><td><span class="ex">used to</span></td><td><span class="ex">There used to be a bakery here.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>add recurring scenes</td><td>habitual <span class="ex">would</span></td><td><span class="ex">Each morning, Lena would unlock the door.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>advance to one event</td><td>Past Simple</td><td><span class="ex">In 2014, a storm damaged the roof.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>close the contrast</td><td>present form</td><td><span class="ex">A language center occupies the building now.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The modal <span class="ex">would</span> has other meanings. In <span class="ex">If the bakery reopened, people would return</span>, it is a hypothetical result. In <span class="ex">Every morning in 2008, people would stop for coffee</span>, it is a past habit. The condition or past-time frame tells the listener which meaning is active.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You established a past state, entered two recurring scenes, advanced to one event, and closed with the present contrast.">
    <h3>Discourse check: complete the four-stage oral history</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Past habit discourse choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="used to be">used to be</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would unlock">would unlock</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="would greet">would greet</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="damaged">damaged</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="occupies">occupies</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Establish the old background and implied change: In 2008, the building <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="used to be" data-hint="Use used to be to establish the former bakery state before the routine begins." data-fix="In 2008, the building used to be a bakery.">choose</button> a bakery.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Enter the established weekday routine: Every morning, Lena <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="would unlock" data-hint="The past world is established, and every morning supplies repeated action." data-fix="Every morning, Lena would unlock the door at six.">choose</button> the door at six.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Add a second recurring scene in that routine: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="would greet" data-hint="Use habitual would for another repeated action in the same clear morning frame." data-fix="She would greet the first commuters by name.">choose</button> the first commuters by name.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Move to one event on September 14, 2014: A storm <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="damaged" data-hint="Use Past Simple for the single dated event that changes the story." data-fix="On September 14, 2014, a storm damaged the roof.">choose</button> the roof.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Close with the situation now: A language center <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="occupies" data-hint="Use Present Simple for the current state marked by now." data-fix="A language center occupies the building now.">choose</button> the building now.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sound natural: <span class="ex">used to</span>, linked questions, and weak <span class="ex">would</span></h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h3>Hear the whole chunk</h3>
      <p>Habitual <span class="ex">used to</span> is commonly /ˈjuːstə/ in connected U.S. speech. The written <span class="ex">d</span> is not pronounced as a separate strong sound before <span class="ex">to</span>. Positive <span class="ex">used to</span> and <span class="ex">use to</span> after <span class="ex">did</span> can sound alike.</p>
      <p><span class="ex">Did you use to...?</span> may link as /ˈdɪdʒə ˈjuːstə/. Use the auxiliary <span class="ex">did</span>, not sound alone, to recover the written question form.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Reduce the modal, stress the memory</h3>
      <p>Habitual <span class="ex">would</span> is often weak /wəd/ or contracts to <span class="ex">’d</span>: <span class="ex">She’d UNLOCK the DOOR at SIX.</span> The repeated action and new detail carry the stronger beats.</p>
      <p>After <span class="ex">’d</span>, a base verb can signal <span class="ex">would</span>: <span class="ex">she’d unlock</span>. A participle can signal <span class="ex">had</span>: <span class="ex">she’d unlocked the door before six</span>. Context and the following verb form resolve the contraction.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read former state, framed repeated action, used-to question, familiarity, and would-versus-had contraction listening check">
